transcriptomics - is a course dedicated  to the statistical analysis of gene  expression data where we explore the  methods of analysis that are typically  used for extracting biological insights  from RNA seek data sets in this session  we will speak about regression as well  as factor analysis in the previous  lesson we discussed the t-test as well  as differential gene expression analysis  we analyzed data by separating the  samples into two comparison groups this  analysis can identify genes that have a  level of expression that is different  between two groups or in other words  differentially expressed genes the  conclusion step of that analysis is  biological interpretation which answers  the question of how differentially  expressed genes show biological  differences between the various  conditions we study however differential  gene expression analysis can be limited  when we have multiple conditions studied  in a dataset for example we can compare  two different conditions but we suspect  that there are additional factors at  play to learn about these types of  relationships let's discuss experimental  design types of variables and methods of  analysis to study their relationships  before this though let's review both the  underlying concepts as well as  terminology we have to understand to  study these kinds of relationships and  our data variables are terms we use to  define our data and experiment and the  external control variable related to  data collection is called an independent  variable is manipulated by what we  intend to study the data we collect are  the dependent variables and these  variables represent the measurements we  take of the conditions in the experiment  oftentimes we would like to establish a  relationship between changes in the  independent variable to establish the  relationships between measuring  dependent variables with the Causby  control sometimes this cause can be  indirect and therefore not a causal  effect even though it appears to be so  nevertheless establishing a non-random  relationship between the two is  important let's say that we collected  data on eye color Jen  age height and the weight of people we  hypothesized that some variables here  are related and some are not we also  think there are exceptions to the rule  the agent height or weight are related  but not always  relationships observed in beta can show  which variables are related unrelated  and if they are related than by how much  weight and height seemed to be logically  related but are they in this data set if  we plot a trend line we will find that  indeed it goes up the higher the height  and weight also increases but the dots  are not too close to the line and some  dots seem to be far away from it is  there a way to quantify this  relationship we have found to study this  relationship we can use a coefficient of  correlation which is also called  r-squared this measure shows how well  data and our data set is explained by  the correlation line drawn through our  data in this case we get an r-squared  coefficient of around 41% 40 is okay but  not perfect our conclusion is that  height and weight are correlated but not  perfectly let's take another example to  see what we can learn from this type of  approach ch Rab P is a gene related to  carbohydrate metabolism if we take  multiple genes that are not related to  carbohydrate intake we will not find the  correlation between intake and their  expressions if we measure this one gene  and carbohydrate intake we can quantify  their relationship by measuring both  insulin secretion as well as the level  of CHR EBP expression these two proteins  are indeed related and their levels are  correlated therefore we can study the  relationship between insulin and gene  expression we can even use this  established relationship between the two  to study additional variables such as  the Fed or fasted conditions  such relationships can be of several  types positive negative and nonlinear  linear regression is a way to further  model such a relationship to determine a  mathematical function thus explaining  the relationship between the dependent  and the independent variable in the  dataset if there is such a relationship  such a function is predictive and will  inform us of how the independent  variable will change if we change the  dependent variable the goal of  regression analysis is to find the right  coefficients of this formula that  minimizes the predictive areas based on  the data we have the regression formula  has both the dependent and independent  variables in a mathematical equation  plus a constant intercept as well as a  coefficient the dependent variable is  related to the independent variable with  additional considerations a constant  intercept or where the line starts from  and the slope of the regression line  which is also known as the coefficient  to get these variables from our data we  can use the least squares approach which  helps measure the distance from each dot  to align finding the line that best fits  the results factor analysis is a method  that uses regression to study  relationships between the data we  measure in the condition we are studying  we are interested to find variation in  the genes associated with the change in  condition as well as the unexpected  variation that comes from the  interaction between factors of the  condition we've already seen how in the  data we have several types of variables  continuous and categorical agent height  our continuous data  where's fasted state our I color a  categorical to study the relationship  between these variables and the results  we should convert the categorical  variables into numbers in the PDX  dataset we have several factors cancer  type and mouse type each one would have  to be represented as a number cancer  type can be er positive her2 negative  her2 positive as well as triple negative  modern approaches the cancer  classification led to further categories  emerging root by whether or not they  have cell receptors for mouse types we  have several types of immunodeficiency  but the level of immune deficiency  varies X ID is characterized by the  absence of the thymus gland and  therefore no t-cell production this  Mouse also has mutant B lymphocytes nod  skid stands for non obese diabetic  severe combined immunodeficiency and  mice of this type lack both mature T and  B cells a thymic nude mice are a  chthonic like XIV so they do not  synthesize t-cells however unlike XIV  they do not have mutated B cells C b-17  skid mice have affected B and T cells  however they have normal amounts of  natural killer cells macrophages as well  as granulocytes let's take a smaller  subset nod 1/8 I make for Mouse type and  ER and T n for cancer type  once our data is labeled let's prepare  the pipeline it is very simple just  connect factor analysis to start then  prepare the groups according to levels  after uploading the files drag the  appropriate name of your sample into the  right group by level and factor  combination er a positive is factor a  level one  hey thymic noon is factor B level one  nod Mouse is factor B level two so the  ER non we will place a macro and so on  when the pipeline is finished we will  get a table of output in this table we  have the gene expression data for all  samples after the last sample there are  three columns with T test results and  after that three columns of F test  results you will notice that there is a  column for each one of the two factors  and an additional column for factor  interaction in many cases when people  talk about the F test what they're  actually talking about is the F test to  compare two variants ease  however the F stat is used in a variety  of tests including regression analysis  the ciao test and the chevy test which  is a post hoc ANOVA test the F test  shows a p-value for the gene expression  relative to the factor is measured for a  low number below 0.